\plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 \sl240 \b \ul 
RUNNING TOOLS3a FOR THE FIRST TIME\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
The first time that you run TOOLS3a, two windows appear.  These are, b
y default, the Internal Disk and Drive B:.  You will see later, that y
ou can change what appears in these windows.\par \pard \plain \s0 
\sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b THE BASICS\par \pard 
\plain \s0 \sl240 
TOOLS3a is a simple program to use.  The basic concept is this:  you h
ave six windows available.  For each window you can specify:\par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Which directory is displayed\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
How this is sorted\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Where the window appears\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
How tall the window is\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Which font is used (and therefore how wide the window is)\par \pard 
\plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 For each file, you can:
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab Copy, Delete, Rename, Move\par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab Set attributes\par \pard \plain \s0 
\sl240 \tab \tab Start with an application (associate)\par \pard 
\plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab Sort (DBF files only)\par \pard \plain 
\s0 \sl240 \tab \tab Edit in Hex mode\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 You can:\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
\tab \tab Create directories\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Remove directories\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Tag files for multiple-file operations\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
\tab \tab Refresh all windows\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Display a directory tree\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard 
\plain \s0 \sl240 New for version 2.1:\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
\tab \tab Change Drive\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Search for Files\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab \tab 
Disk Information\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s1 
\sb240 \sl240 \b \ul FILE OPERATIONS\par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 
\sl240 \b COPY\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Move to the file you wish to copy.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 2.\tab Select 'copy' from the menu.\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 3.\tab 
You may now choose a window to copy this file to.  If you wish to c
opy this file to somewhere else, then press ESC, and you can enter the
 full specification of the destination file.\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b MOVE
\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Move to the file you wish to move.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 2.\tab Select 'move' from the menu.\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 3.\tab 
You may now choose a window to move this file to.  If you wish to m
ove this file to somewhere else, then press ESC, and you can enter the
 full specification of the new filename.\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b RENAME
\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Move to the file you wish to rename.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 2.\tab Select 'rename' from the menu.\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 3.\tab 
Enter the new name for this file.  If you change the directory name
, then you actually move this file to the new directory.\par \pard 
\plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 
\sl240 \b DELETE\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Move to the file you wish to delete.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 2.\tab Select 'delete' from the menu.\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 3.\tab 
You will be asked to confirm that this is the correct file to delete.
\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 
\sb120 \sl240 \b ATTRIBUTES\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 
\sl240 1.\tab Move to the file you wish to set the attributes of.\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 2.\tab 
Select 'Attribute' from the menu.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 3.\tab You may now change the attributes of the file.
\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \tab 
(Read Only, Modified, Hidden, System).\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 \b \par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \b 
TOOLS3a\plain 
 shows all files, regardless of whether or not they are Hidden.\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 
\sl240 \b TAG\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Move to the file you wish to tag.  Selecting 'Tag' toggles the tagg
ing on this file.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \tab 
+ and - will set and cancel the Tag.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 \tab 
* tags all files in the current window, / removes all tags in the cur
rent window.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 2.\tab 
You can now select the operation you wish to perform: Copy, Move, o
r Delete.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard 
\plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i 
Note: You can also tag files by pressing Shift-Up or Shift-Down.\par 
\pard \plain \s1 \sb240 \sl240 \b \ul \par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 
\sl240 \b \ul DIRECTORY OPERATIONS\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par 
\pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b CREATE DIRECTORY\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Select 'Create Directory' from the menu.\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 2.\tab 
You can then edit the name of the directory displayed, to specify t
he name of the new directory.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 
\sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b REMOVE DIRECTORY\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 1.\tab 
Move to the directory to be removed.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 2.\tab Select 'Remove Directory' from the menu.\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 3.\tab 
You must now confirm the name of the directory to remove.\par \pard 
\plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 \i 
Note: All files and sub-directories within this directory will be dele
ted.\par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 \sl240 \b \ul FILE ASSOCIATIONS\par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Pressing RETURN whilst positioned on a directory enters this directory
.  On a file, this starts the file with the associated application.
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
For example, if you are on a file .WRD, then the word processor will s
tart with this file.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain 
\s0 \sl240 
If you have your own applications, you can set up the association usin
g the 'Associate' option on the menu.  You then need to supply the fol
lowing data:\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i Extension\tab \plain 
This is the extension to be associated, eg. PRN\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i Application\tab \plain 
The name of the application file, eg. ROM::WORD.APP\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i Command\tab \plain 
This is used by the system to assign the function keys, so use\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \tab \tab \tab 
"Word", if you wish to be able to select this file with the Word key.
\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i Alias\tab \tab \tab 
\plain 
Used by Word for plain text files.  For plain text, set this to $\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i OPA\tab \tab \plain \tab 
This is only needed if you write your own application files in OPL.
\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
To run an OPA, you need to set the "Application" field to ROM::SYS$PRG
O.IMG\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
and the name of your application must go in the "OPA" field with the F
ULL file\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 specification.
\par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 \sl240 \b \ul DISPLAY OPTIONS\par \pard 
\plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b SORTING\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
To sort the file list, select 'Sort' from the menu.\par \pard \plain 
\s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
You may sort in Name, Extension, Size, or Date order, or leave the lis
t unsorted.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard 
\plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b ZOOMING\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 
You may zoom the window in and out, in order to change the font size, 
and therefore the width of the window.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b NEW WINDOW\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
You may use up to six windows simultaneously.  This option creates a n
ew window.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
You must specify the path which is to be used.\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \i 
Note: To change the path in a window, press PSION-TAB.\par \pard 
\plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 
\sl240 \b MOVE WINDOW\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
You can move the window in all four directions using the cursor keys. 
 Pressing SHIFT moves the window faster.  In general, you cannot move 
completely off the screen, nor can you overlap with the status window.
  Pressing ENTER confirms the new position.  Pressing ESC returns to t
he old position.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par 
\pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b SIZE WINDOW\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
Using the up and down cursor keys, you can change the height of the wi
ndow.  The width is fixed by the font used.  Again, pressing SHIFT mov
es faster, ENTER confirms, and ESC cancels.\par \pard \plain \s20 
\fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b 
CLOSE WINDOW\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
Here you can close one of your windows.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 
\li1134 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b ZOOMING\par 
\pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
Zooming changes the size of the font used, and therefore the width of 
the window.\par \pard \plain \s20 \fi-567 \li1134 \sl240 
Each window has its own font setting.\par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 
\sl240 \b \ul THE TOOLS\par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b 
DBF TOOLS\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
DBF Tools allows you to sort your database files, and also to insert a
 field into a database file.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
You should first be on a valid DBF file before you choose this option,
 and the file must be NOT be open by any other application (eg. Data).
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
You are given the following options:\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab 
\tab \tab \tab \tab Insert Field\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \tab 
\tab \tab \tab \tab Sort File\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard 
\plain \s0 \sl240 
Using "Insert Field" you can insert a text field at a specified positi
on.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Using "Sort File" you can sort the file based on the contents of one o
f its fields.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 
\sl240 
These routines are in principle the same as the example programs in th
e OPL handbook, but have been slightly modified for added functionalit
y.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b 
HEX EDITOR\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
The hex editor allows low-level access to the Psion File Structures.  
You are shown your chosen file in both HEX and ASCII views.  Be defaul
t you are in 'Read-Only' mode, but by turning off the Protection you c
an also edit the contents of the file.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Using 'Address On' you can display the current address in the file.
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
You can search for a string, or jump to an address.  You may also open
 a different file if you wish.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard 
\plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b REFRESH\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
This option will refresh the information in all of the windows.\par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b 
DIRECTORY TREE\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
This option displays a tree diagram of the currently selected drive.  
You can navigate up and down, and then press ESC to quit, or ENTER to 
select the new sub-directory.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard 
\plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b CHANGE DRIVE\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
This options presents a dialog containing a list of the available driv
es, from which you may choose one.  The current window will then switc
h to this drive.\par \pard \plain \s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b \line 
FILE SEARCH\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
File search requires you to enter the part of the filename you wish to
 search for \plain \i 
(Wildcards are not allowed.  To search for FILE*.* type FILE).\plain 
  You also need to specify where to look.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Current Path searches in the directory of the current window.\par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Current Drive searches in all directories of the drive in the current 
window.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Local Drives searches in all directories on LOC::A, LOC::B, LOC::M, et
c.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
All Drives searches in all directories on all available drives, eg. LO
C::A, ..., REM::A, ...\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain 
\s2 \sb120 \sl240 \b DISK INFORMATION\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
Disk information provides details of the type of disk in use and how m
uch space is available.\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain 
\s0 \sl240 
Pressing ESCAPE will return to the main program.  Pressing any other k
ey will start a scan of the current disk.  This then reports the numbe
r of files on the disk with how much space they occupy.  From this it 
is possible to see how much space would be freed by formatting a flash
 using the formula:\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 
\sl240 \tab \tab 
(Total Disk Space - Space Free) - Total Space Occupied by files\par 
\pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
This report also shows how many of the files have their 'modified' bit
 set.  If the 'Backup' option in the System menu is used, or the -M pa
rameter in MCLINK, then this will show the number of files which are n
ot backed up.  A percentage is also shown.\par \pard \plain \s0 
\sl240 \par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 \sl240 \b \ul TECHNICAL SUPPORT
\line \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
If you have any problems using TOOLS3a, then Technical Support is avai
lable, using one of the methods outlined above.\par \pard \plain \s0 
\sl240 \par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 
To perform a 'reset' of TOOLS3a, you should delete the TOOLS3A.INI and
 ASSOC.INI files in the \\TOOLS\\ directory of your internal disk.
\par \pard \plain \s0 \sl240 \par \pard \plain \s1 \sb240 \sl240 \b 
